{"description":"persona-grpc/tests/e2e.rs:59-66:

    fn tmp_socket_path() -> String {
        let nanos = SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap().subsec_nanos();
        format!("/tmp/persona-test-{nanos}.sock")
    }

Only the sub-second component of the clock is used, so the generated name repeats every second and is not scoped to the process. Two `cargo test` runs on the same machine — a CI matrix sharing a runner, or a developer running tests while CI does — can land on the same path.

The collision does not surface as an error. persona-grpc/src/server.rs:18-21 deletes any existing socket at the path and rebinds:

    if socket_path.exists() {
        tracing::warn!(?socket_path, "removing stale socket");
        std::fs::remove_file(socket_path)?;
    }

So the second run silently steals the first run's socket, and the first run's client fails on an RPC with an error that points nowhere near the cause.

Fix: include `std::process::id()` and a per-test atomic counter, and use `std::env::temp_dir()` rather than a hardcoded `/tmp`.

Scoped to the path collision only. The 100ms readiness sleep and the socket leaked on panic are persona-5s4b.61.","title":"e2e test socket path collides across concurrent runs"}

